Biological activity soil was evaluated 85 days post-sowing by obtaining soil samples to quantify CO2 efflux
and microbial biomass C, adhering to methodologies described by43specifically utilizing fumigation incubation–
extraction technique. Quantifying microbial biomass C in the soil is crucial, as it functions as a sign of microbial
community’s size and its function in OM dynamics and nutrient cycling44. Furthermore, the activities of soil
enzymes were assessed 85 days post-sowing by collecting soil samples to quantify alkaline phosphatase and
dehydrogenase activities. Dehydrogenase function in soil is crucial since it acts as a primary indication of
microbial oxidative activities and total soil biological health. The activity of alkaline phosphatase in the soil
is significant as it indicates soil’s ability to mineralize organic P into accessible forms for crop uptake45. The
activity of dehydrogenase was quantified utilizing spectrophotometer (YR06035) according to [68] employing 2,
3, 5-TTC technique (3% w/v), while activity of alkaline phosphatase was assessed with 4-nitrophenyl phosphate
as a substrate, with absorbance recorded at 440 nm, as outlined by George et al.46. The bacteriological activities
were assessed by quantifying the total bacterial count, Bacillus, and Azotobacter in soil samples at 75 days,
utilizing King’s B agar medium, according to methodologies outlined by Kizilkaya47. Assessing Bacillus and
Azotobacter in the soil is crucial, as they serve as vital signs of microbial health and facilitate nitrogen fixation,
nutrient cycling, and the promotion of plant growth48. During the harvest, soil samples were obtained to assess
Pb concentration, extracted with EDTA, and measured via AAS (AAS 700, Perkin Elmer).

